  "narrative": "No assessments are due, so this stage registers new comparisons. The current strongest supplied-dataset associations are TP53 loss with weaker composite dependency, KEAP1 loss with weaker composite dependency, SMARCA4 loss with stronger composite dependency, and PTEN loss with stronger composite dependency in a TP53/KEAP1/SMARCA4-wildtype background. The main scientific question now is whether these accepted associations are broadly consistent across biologically and assay-relevant contexts, or whether they are context-dependent. I therefore register interactions for the accepted predictors with ploidy, proliferation rate, media serum concentration, and selected transcriptional-state thresholds. These are not tests of whether the main effects exist; they test whether the exposure-composite dependency contrast differs by at least the pre-specified 0.15-unit scale between high-context and complement cells. A threshold-level signed interaction would indicate context dependence; a near-null interaction would indicate that the main association is similar across that context; an opposite-direction threshold interaction would indicate attenuation or reversal, but only if interpreted carefully rather than as invalidating the main effect. For TP53 and KEAP1, the registered positive interaction direction means the raw exposed-minus-comparator contrast is larger in the high-context group than in the complement; because these predictors have positive main contrasts associated with weaker dependency, a positive interaction would mean a stronger weaker-dependency effect in the high context. For SMARCA4 and PTEN clean-background tests, the registered negative main-effect direction means a positive signed interaction corresponds to a more negative raw contrast, i.e. stronger dependency, in the high-context group. I include high ploidy and high growth because composite dependency could vary with chromosomal instability and proliferation. I include serum concentration because culture condition may modify fitness effects and composite dependency scoring. I include high EMT, hypoxia, and stemness as broad biological-state contexts: TP53 effects may be modified by epithelial plasticity, KEAP1 effects by oxidative-stress or hypoxic stress context, and SMARCA4 effects by chromatin/plasticity state. Because these are constructed or continuous assays with uncertain causal roles, I pre-register them as uncertain modifications rather than expecting a particular direction. For PTEN, the accepted effect was estimated in a relatively clean genetic background, so I register a ploidy interaction only in that clean background to test whether the PTEN association is stable or chromosomal-instability dependent. Finally, I add BRCA2 loss as a broad screen in the stronger-dependency direction. DNA-repair gene losses can sometimes create selective vulnerabilities, but they can also have small or nonspecific effects in composite dependency scores. The comparison requires at least 20 exposed and comparator cells and is expected to have adequate sample size. All claims remain associations in the supplied dataset, not causal statements or generalized biomarker claims.",
