pposite direction, and neither interval is wholly above the threshold. This does not support a scientifically meaningful Avana-library main effect, so I maintain reject and close.\n\nOverall supplied-dataset conclusions are now: TP53 loss is associated with weaker composite dependency (H13/R1+R26, replicated and stable across measured contexts including ploidy strata H102/H103 and serum strata H95/H96); KEAP1 loss is associated with weaker composite dependency (H3/R3+R13, stable across ploidy H104/H105 and serum H93/H94); SMARCA4 loss is associated with stronger composite dependency (H4/R4+R39, strong in clean TP53/KEAP1 background H30/R16+R40, and stable across ploidy H100/H101 and serum H97/H98); PTEN loss is associated with stronger composite dependency only in the TP53/KEAP1/SMARCA4-wildtype background (H67/R54+R66), and this is not an unqualified full-population PTEN claim; MSI-high is associated with stronger composite dependency (H111/R105+R106). Negative or unsupported findings include the research markers, STK11, MTAP, KRAS, ARID1A, ERBB2, MYC, PIK3CA, RB1, APC, SMAD4, BRCA2, NF1, BRAF, and the Avana-library main effect, plus rejected serum/lineage/Cas9/growth-rate/culture/stemness context modifiers where intervals were clear enough to reject. Unresolved uncertainty remains mainly around CDKN2A, full-population PTEN, EGFR amplification, and pending technical library checks. The most informative next work is to test whether the newly validated MSI-high association is stable in clean TP53/KEAP1/SMARCA4 background and across ploidy strata, while retaining Ky and Humagne-CD library checks as low-priority artifact screens.",
