  "narrative": "The due validation response is H133: PTEN loss versus no PTEN loss among MSI-stable samples, with direction -1 so a positive signed estimate means the raw exposed-minus-comparator contrast is negative, i.e. lower composite dependency score and stronger dependency. Discovery R130 gives +0.254 [0.159, 0.349], entirely above the 0.15-unit scientific-meaningfulness threshold. Independent validation R132 gives +0.239 [0.108, 0.371]; the point estimate remains well above threshold and in the same direction, although the conservative independent interval crosses below the threshold. The validation interval is very wide because it accounts for the maximum number of validation comparisons, and its lower bound still remains far from zero. Taken together with the discovery interval, the consistency of direction, and the accepted PTEN clean-context family (for example H121, H145, and H146), I accept and close H133 as a supplied-dataset association: PTEN loss is associated with stronger dependency among MSI-stable samples. This does not prove a causal mechanism, does not imply uniform PTEN effects across every co-mutational background, and does not imply external generalizability.\n\nThe final evidence supports a small set of robust supplied-dataset associations. TP53 loss is associated with higher composite scores, i.e. weaker dependency, with discovery and validation support in H1/H13. KEAP1 loss is similarly associated with higher scores and weaker dependency, with validation in H3. SMARCA4 loss is associated with lower scores and stronger dependency, validated in H4 and supported by multiple context analyses including H30. MSI-high is associated with very strong lower scores/stronger dependency and is the most robust effect in the ledger; it persists after accounting for co-occurring TP53/KEAP1/SMARCA4/MTAP/CDKN2A wildtype background, outside the top mutation-burden quartile, in lower-aneuploidy samples, high screen-quality samples, adherent culture, and after independent validation (H111/R105/R106, H147, H150, H152, H154, H155).\n\nPTEN loss now has a coherent accepted association in MSI-stable samples through H133 and also in several clean genomic contexts such as H121 and H145/H146. The broad PTEN main effect remains accepted in the maintained ledger (H58), but because PTEN was tested across many context cuts, the most defensible interpretation is a context-associated PTEN dependency difference rather than a uniform biological law across all genomic backgrounds.\n\nNegative or unsupported findings are extensive. The constructed research markers D/E/F show no threshold-level main effects. Most conventional driver or loss variables show no scientifically meaningful associations: KRAS, PIK3CA, APC, SMAD4, NF1, ARID1A, MYC amplification, ERBB2 amplification, BRAF V600E, RB1 loss, BRCA2 loss, and others are rejected or remain far below threshold. STK11 loss and MTAP loss do not show accepted threshold-level main effects. Basic omics-completeness indicators and CRISPR library main effects are not supported. Most tested interactions do not establish threshold-level modification: lineage subtype, TP53/KEAP1/SMARCA4 co-states, ploidy, growth rate, CAS9 activity, media serum, EMT/hypoxia/stemness, mutation burden, screen batch, and most marker/signature interactions are rejected or unresolved without a reliable threshold-level effect.\n\nThe main unresolved uncertainty is CDKN2A loss. Its point estimates often sit near the 0.15 threshold, but intervals repeatedly include below-threshold values and no available evidence gives a robust threshold-level association. I therefore do not accept CDKN2A and avoid further arbitrary context cuts. Other unresolved items are low-priority modifiers with imprecise intervals, such as some PTEN co-deletion contexts, high-aneuploidy MSI interaction, batch-related SMARCA4 interaction, and unanalyzed signature-modifier tests. The most informative next analysis is a newly registered refinement: PTEN loss in a fully clean TP53/KEAP1/SMARCA4/MSI/MTAP/CDKN2A wildtype background. This tests whether the accepted PTEN signal survives removal of CDKN2A co-deletion context, the most relevant remaining confounder among accepted borderline predictors. No further validation requests remain, so any subsequent conclusions remain supplied-dataset associations, not causal or external claims.",
  "research_notes": "Iteration 12 synthesize final. H133 is accepted and closed: PTEN loss among MSI-stable samples shows signed +0.254 [0.159,0.349] discovery and independent validation +0.239 [0.108,0.371]; positive signed means raw lower dependency score/stronger dependency. The validation lower bound crosses the 0.15 threshold under a very conservative interval, but the point estimate, discovery interval, and other PTEN clean-context accepted lines support accepting H133 as a supplied-dataset association.\n\nFinal supported supplied-dataset associations: TP53 loss -> higher composite score/weaker dependency; KEAP1 loss -> higher composite score/weaker dependency; SMARCA4 loss -> lower composite score/stronger dependency; MSI-high -> very strong lower composite score/stronger dependency, highly robust across strata and validated; PTEN loss -> lower composite score/stronger dependency in MSI-stable H133 and clean genomic contexts (H121, H145, H146, and related accepted cuts). The broad PTEN main effect remains accepted in the maintained ledger but should be interpreted with context heterogeneity in mind.\n\nNegative/unsupported: constructed markers, most conventional driver/loss/amplicon variables, STK11, MTAP main effect, omics-completeness indicators, library main effects, and most interaction modifiers. No robust threshold-level modifier was supported.\n\nUnresolved: CDKN2A loss remains borderline and not accepted; some PTEN co-deletion contexts and modifier interactions remain imprecise; deferred signature-interaction and PTEN-high-aneuploidy tests remain unresolved/deferred. New refinement registered: PTEN loss in TP53/KEAP1/SMARCA4/MSI/MTAP/CDKN2A wildtype background to test separation from CDKN2A. No new validation requests remain; final claims are dataset associations without causality or external generalizability."
