  "narrative": "I assessed all newly returned discovery evidence against the pre-registered claim direction and the 0.15 absolute composite dependency-score threshold. Returned estimates are already signed to the hypothesis direction: for direction -1, a positive signed estimate means the exposed group has a lower composite dependency score, i.e. stronger dependency; for direction +1, a positive signed estimate means a higher composite score, i.e. weaker dependency.\n\nH161 is accepted and closed: in the fully clean genomic/MSI background (TP53, KEAP1, SMARCA4, MSI, MTAP, and CDKN2A wildtype/reference states), PTEN loss has signed estimate +0.2901 with 95% interval [0.1909, 0.3893], entirely above the 0.15-unit threshold in the claimed stronger-dependency direction. This is the strongest interval-level PTEN context evidence so far and argues against the signal being driven by overlap with TP53/KEAP1/SMARCA4/MTAP/CDKN2A or MSI co-states. H162 is accepted and closed: TP53 loss remains associated with weaker dependency among SMARCA4-loss samples, signed +0.8973 [0.7892, 1.0054], far above threshold.\n\nThe high-screen-quality robustness tests are strong for the three large main-effect predictors. H165 accepts TP53 loss under higher screen ROC quality, signed +0.8379 [0.7530, 0.9229]. H166 accepts KEAP1 loss under the same condition, signed +0.7724 [0.6784, 0.8664]. H167 accepts SMARCA4 loss under the same condition, signed +0.8057 [0.7220, 0.8893]. These intervals are clearly above threshold and reduce concern that the accepted TP53, KEAP1, or SMARCA4 associations are artifacts of lower-quality screens, though they remain supplied-dataset associations.\n\nH168 accepts MSI-high within the high mutation-burden subset, signed +0.7450 [0.5826, 0.9073]. Together with earlier evidence that MSI-high persists outside the high mutation-burden tail and in several independent contexts, this supports a broad and robust MSI-high association rather than one tied specifically to high