 "narrative": "No assessments are due and voluntary validation requests are exhausted, so this explore stage registers focused discovery refinements that can still change the supplied-dataset conclusions. The highest priority is CDKN2A loss. It is the main unresolved predictor: many context-specific estimates cluster around +0.13 to +0.19 signed units, but lower bounds remain below the pre-specified +0.15 composite-unit threshold. I therefore split the fully clean MTAP-negative, MSI-stable, TP53/KEAP1/SMARCA4-intact context into high versus low screen_roc_auc strata. The scientific hypothesis is that CDKN2A loss is associated with a lower composite dependency score, i.e. stronger dependency, so the raw exposed-minus-comparator contrast is expected to be negative and the signed estimate is expected to be positive. Because screen_roc_auc is a measured screen-quality variable, higher-quality screens may reduce measurement noise and improve the chance of resolving a near-threshold biological association; alternatively, any apparent CDKN2A effect could be concentrated in lower-quality screens, could be diluted by screen-quality heterogeneity, or could remain below scientific meaning. Each stratum will be accepted only if the signed 95% Welch lower bound exceeds +0.15. If neither does, the CDKN2A branch can be closed as practically uncertain or below the threshold rather than continued with additional arbitrary cuts. The second focus is completing the reciprocal co-genomic robustness checks for the three main genomic associations. SMARCA4 loss is accepted as a strong association with lower composite scores, meaning stronger dependency, and KEAP1 loss is accepted as a strong association with higher composite scores, meaning weaker dependency. I register SMARCA4 loss among TP53-intact KEAP1-loss cells to test whether the SMARCA4 stronger-dependency association persists when KEAP1 loss is present. The expected raw contrast is negative, giving a positive signed estimate under direction -1. The alternatives are no additional SMARCA4 effect, attenuation by KEAP1-loss biology, or imprecision. I also register KEAP1 loss among SMARCA4-intact TP53-loss cells to test whether KEAP1 loss remains associated with weaker dependency when TP53 loss is present. The expected raw contrast is positive and signed positive under direction +1, with alternatives of context-specific attenuation or no independent effect. These comparisons are direct mean differences, not reusing the failed interaction claims, and they are intended to test persistence of accepted main effects in biologically coherent co-genomic backgrounds rather than to assert causal interaction. The third focus is PTEN loss, which is supported as a moderate stronger-dependency association mainly in genomic-clean contexts but remains less robust than the four main predictors. I register PTEN loss among SMARCA4-loss cells with TP53/KEAP1 intact and MSI/MTAP stable to ask whether PTEN loss still points in the lower-score direction when SMARCA4 loss is present. The raw contrast is expected to be negative, signed positive under direction -1. I also register PTEN loss among TP53-loss cells with KEAP1/SMARCA4 intact and MSI/MTAP stable to test whether the near-threshold PTEN signal observed in broader TP53-loss cells persists after reducing co-genomic noise. The same alternatives apply: a threshold-supported negative raw contrast would extend PTEN context coverage, while interval-limited or opposite-direction results would leave PTEN context-specific and unresolved. Finally, I register MSI-high among TP53-loss cells to test whether the accepted strong MSI-high stronger-dependency association persists when the TP53-loss background is present. This is a smaller-cell comparison and may be imprecise or invalid if any cell is below 20, but it is a useful co-genomic boundary: if accepted, it supports MSI-high as a predictor beyond TP53-intact contexts; if rejected or imprecise, the MSI-high conclusion remains mainly a supplied-dataset association not established within TP53-loss. All returned estimates are already signed to the registered direction, so they will not be multiplied again by direction. These proposals remain associations in the supplied dataset; causality, mechanism, and external generalizability are not claimed.",
