  "narrative": "I assessed the newly delivered discovery evidence against the pre-specified 0.15 composite-unit threshold and the pre-registered hypothesis direction. All estimates are already signed to the claim direction; a positive signed estimate supports the registered direction. For direction +1, this corresponds to a higher composite score and therefore weaker dependency; for direction -1, it corresponds to a lower composite score and stronger dependency. Independent validation cannot be requested because the voluntary validation budget is exhausted.\n\nThe co-genomic persistence checks for the main genomic predictors are strong. H16 tests KEAP1 loss among STK11-wild-type cells: signed estimate +0.808 [0.735, 0.882], n=318 exposed and 1301 comparator, with lower bound far above threshold. I accept this in the supplied dataset: KEAP1 loss remains associated with a higher composite score, i.e. weaker dependency, even when STK11 loss is absent. H19 tests SMARCA4 loss among STK11-wild-type cells: signed estimate +0.768 [0.697, 0.839], direction -1, so the raw contrast is a lower composite score, i.e. stronger dependency. Its lower bound is far above threshold, so I accept it. H21 tests TP53 loss among SMARCA4-wild-type cells: signed estimate +0.828 [0.769, 0.886], lower bound far above threshold, accepted. H46 tests TP53 loss among STK11-wild-type cells: signed estimate +0.808 [0.739, 0.876], accepted. These are within-dataset persistence associations, not causal interaction claims or external replication, but they reduce concern that the main TP53, KEAP1, or SMARCA4 signals are carried only by STK11 or SMARCA4 co-genomic subsets.\n\nH48 tests whether the STK11-loss association is modified by KEAP1-loss status. The signed interaction is -0.011 [-0.172, 0.151]. Although the upper bound reaches the threshold by a trivial margin, the estimate is near zero and opposite the registered direction, and the interval is centered well below any scientifically meaningful positive modification. I reject the claimed-direction STK11-by-KEAP1-loss interaction. This is consistent with the broader pattern of no reliable modification of the main genomic associations by the tested context variables.\n\nThe direct media-serum baseline comparisons are not threshold-level in the registered direction. H201, media 10% versus 5%, gives signed -0.043 [-0.114, 0.028], opposite the claimed positive direction and well below threshold. H202, media 10% versus 2%, gives +0.003 [-0.076, 0.083], essentially null. H203, media 5% versus 2%, gives +0.046 [-0.047, 0.139], below threshold and not interval-supported. I reject all three as scientifically meaningful direct media effects in this dataset. This does not prove exact equality of media conditions; it means none reaches the pre-specified effect-size standard with interval-level support.\n\nH206 tests whether MSI-high's stronger-dependency association is modified by high IFN-gamma signature. The signed interaction is +0.123 [-0.104, 0.351]. The point estimate is in the claimed direction but below threshold, the lower bound does not provide interval-level support, and the upper bound leaves a threshold-level modification possible. Given the small exposed interaction cell and the large number of interaction hypotheses tested, I cannot accept this modification. I leave it unresolved but close it because no pre-specified independent validation pathway remains and further cuts would raise multiplicity risk without strong prior justification.\n\nThe reciprocal PTEN and CDKN2A co-loss boundary checks failed the minimum cell-size rule. H204 has insufficient cell_n [18, 88], and H205 has insufficient cell_n [18, 145]. These are not valid comparisons and therefore provide no evidence either way. I keep both claims unresolved in scientific interpretation but close the specific small co-loss branches because they were pre-specified boundary checks and the required cells are too sparse for a valid Welch comparison. This