terval (lower +0.017, upper +0.602). None provides 95%-interval evidence that the association exceeds the prespecified meaningful-effect threshold. I do not reject CDKN2A loss because all point estimates are in the claimed direction and several upper bounds are well above threshold, but the supplied-dataset evidence is insufficient to accept any CDKN2A loss comparison as scientifically meaningful. These branches are closed as in-dataset tests; external adjusted replication would be needed to resolve them.\n\nThe new PTEN boundary tests also remain unresolved. H231, PTEN loss within CDKN2A-loss-positive MSI-stable cells, has a large point estimate +0.330 but lower bound +0.101, below threshold and based on a small exposed cell (n=47). H232, PTEN loss within TP53-wildtype, MSI-stable, high-mutation-burden cells, gives +0.186 but lower bound -0.010, so the interval is compatible with no effect or the opposite direction. I do not accept these as supported threshold-level effects, nor do I reject them as absence of effect because the intervals are wide and the point estimates are generally positive. This supports context dependence for PTEN loss: the stronger-dependency pattern is best supported in cleaner/lower-mutation-burden contexts, while high-mutation-burden and sparse co-loss contexts remain uncertain.\n\nNo validation request is made because the voluntary validation budget is exhausted. The accepted new claims remain discoveries in the supplied dataset. Multiplicity is large, estimates are unadjusted, and the observed differences may be confounded by correlated genomic features or technical study factors. Biological interpretation and external generalizability remain uncertain.",
