n hypotheses test whether MYC, ASCL2, or AXL expression modifies core TP53, KEAP1, SMARCA4, or MSI-high associations. MYC is a plausible modifier because it alters proliferation, metabolism, replication stress, and DNA-damage responses; ASCL2 may mark stem or lineage-program states that could alter chromatin-remodeler dependency; AXL may mark mesenchymal or EMT-like states that could modify stress-response dependencies. These interactions are falsifiable and allow discovery of context specificity, but they are underpowered relative to main-effect tests and may remain rejected or unresolved even if biologically real.  The CDKN2A MYC-high comparison revisits the main unresolved predictor in a biologically motivated context. CDKN2A loss may have a stronger cell-cycle dependency association when MYC activity is high, but the subgroup effect has smaller sample size than the overall contrast, so acceptance will require a clear signed lower bound above +0.15.  All proposed comparisons remain crude unadjusted associations in the supplied dataset. They do not establish causality, mechanism, or external generalizability, and the run has already involved many Welch comparisons. Validation cannot be requested because the voluntary validation budget is exhausted.",
