  "narrative": "No validations are due and no new voluntary validation budget remains, so this explore stage registers final in-dataset specificity and robustness tests rather than attempting more validation. The accepted core findings remain TP53 loss and KEAP1 loss associated with higher dependency_composite, i.e. weaker dependency, and SMARCA4 loss and MSI-high associated with lower dependency_composite, i.e. stronger dependency, with large validated effects far above the 0.15 composite-unit threshold. PTEN loss is best interpreted as a moderate context-dependent association, strongest in high-ASCL2 or restricted genomic backgrounds, while CDKN2A loss remains persistently near but usually below the 0.15 threshold. The first four proposals test the core predictors in the ASCL2-low complement. This asks whether high ASCL2 is a special context or whether the accepted main effects are broadly present across expression strata. The PTEN-low-ASCL2 clean-background proposal tests the complement of the accepted high-ASCL2 PTEN signal while removing major correlated losses that could inflate a broad subgroup estimate; it addresses whether the low-ASCL2 unresolved result reflects weak biology or confounding/selection. The PTEN-high-ASCL2 clean background also excluding CDKN2A tests whether the accepted high-ASCL2 PTEN signal is independent of the unresolved CDKN2A effect and of correlated core losses. The CDKN2A low 9p21 copy-number-background proposal tests a mechanistically coherent p16-locus context: if CDKN2A loss has a real modest effect, it may be stronger where 9p21 copy-number loss is present. All returned estimates will be signed to the registered hypothesis direction, and acceptance will require valid Welch cells and a signed lower bound above +0.15 composite dependency-score units. Null or unresolved results should be read as insufficient evidence in this dataset for the proposed context-specific effect, not as proof of absence. These remain unadjusted associations in the supplied dataset and do not establish causality or generalizability.",
  "research_notes": "Final-run notes: accepted supplied-dataset core associations are TP53 loss and KEAP1 loss associated with higher dependency_composite/weaker dependency, and SMARCA4 loss and MSI-high associated with lower dependency_composite/stronger dependency; these are large, stable, and independently validated within this evaluation framework. PTEN loss is moderate and context-dependent, with strongest evidence in high-ASCL2 or restricted clean backgrounds, but broad PTEN and PTEN-interaction evidence are imprecise or negative. CDKN2A loss remains unresolved near the 0.15 threshold. Negative findings include most other genomic mutations/amplifications/losses, research markers, libraries, omics availability, media-serum means, sex, and most interaction modifiers; rejected interactions indicate lack of robust support for modification, not opposite direction. The new proposals are final specificity tests: core effects in ASCL2-low, PTEN in low-ASCL2 clean background, PTEN in high-ASCL2 clean background excluding CDKN2A, and CDKN2A in low 9p21 copy-number background. They aim to distinguish broad versus expression-context effects and to probe whether CDKN2A becomes detectable in a p16-locus-like genomic context. External pre-specified multivariable replication remains the next step for causal or generalizable claims."
